<description>
`EventLoop::loop()` exits when its reference count reaches zero. In `serve()` mode, the parent connection is the only reference keeping the loop running. Other `CapnpProtocol` methods called from threads not handling parent IPC requests do not hold their own references and therefore depend on the parent connection remaining alive.

Add `loopRef()` (code suggested by @ryanofsky in https://github.com/bitcoin-core/libmultiprocess/pull/376#discussion_r4268952676), which returns a new reference to the event loop or throws if the loop is not running or is shutting down, and use it in the methods that need the loop. Protect `m_loop` with a new `m_loop_mutex` to prevent `serve()` from destroying the loop while `loopRef()` is accessing it.
</description>

<commits>
dcae543524 ipc: hold event loop references when using it in CapnpProtocol

EventLoop::loop() exits when its reference count reaches zero. In serve()
mode, the parent connection is the only reference keeping the loop running.
Other CapnpProtocol methods called from threads not handling parent IPC
requests do not hold their own references and therefore depend on the
parent connection remaining alive.

Add loopRef(), which returns a new reference to the event loop or throws
if the loop is not running or is shutting down, and use it in the methods
that need the loop. Protect m_loop with a new m_loop_mutex to prevent
serve() from destroying the loop while loopRef() is accessing it.

Co-authored-by: Ryan Ofsky <ryan@ofsky.org>
</commits>

